# Target C-II — dominant atomic train orbit forces outer directional energy

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `11b96ae4d7ec5261efb4ebca3d9baf04444c48c4`  
**Status:** proof-only low-`t` Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** full-core/no-container Type-II branch, using AG42 and the audited §142 train-phase stack  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current low-`t` endpoint has two features that have so far been used separately:

1. the strict-rhombus train crossing measure is almost concentrated in one
   `pi/3` atomic orbit when the all-train scalar `C=(T-q/2)/D` is close to its
   AG42 floor; and
2. the loaded force hull is asymptotically round, while its occurrence-counted outer
   contact routes have very small total directional energy
   `d_out=b-per(F)`.

These features are incompatible at the old endpoint.  A single three-direction atomic
orbit cannot supply the required number of outer train-terminal occurrences around a
round hull without paying a definite angular-energy cost.

The resulting scalar exclusion raises the proof-only low-`t` floor from

\[
t_{42}=1.8208886379794447\ldots
\]

to the safe explicit bound

\[
\boxed{\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge 1.82428.}
\]

The scalar root of the present argument is

\[
1.8242812351707521\ldots,
\]

but only the safely certified round value `1.82428` is used as a theorem constant.

---

## 1. Normalized low-`t` variables

Pass to a convergent full-core Type-II subsequence and write

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}
={b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}.
\]

Let

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\qquad
C_0=2s_2+0.00042.
\]

As in the low-`t` network theorem, put

\[
C:={T-q/2\over D}.
\]

Using `T-q=2f-k`, this is exactly the scalar already used in the §171 ledgers:

\[
C={t\over2}+2{f\over D}-{\kappa\over2}.
\]

AG42 gives

\[
\boxed{C\ge C_0.}
\tag{OE.1}
\]

The audited §171 upper envelope gives

\[
\boxed{
C\le U:= {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2}.
}
\tag{OE.2}
\]

Hence `U>=C0`, equivalently

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le R(t),
\qquad
R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
\tag{OE.3}
\]

---

## 2. One atomic orbit contains almost all train mass at the endpoint

Use the audited §142 train-phase notation.  Let

\[
x={T\over D},
\qquad
\sigma=\text{total atomic mass of the limiting projective train-phase measure},
\]

and let

\[
A:=x\sigma
\]

be the absolute atomic train mass per unit diameter.

The exposed-hull inequality from §142 is

\[
{q/T}\le2(1-\sigma).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
A=x\sigma
\le x-{q\over2D}
=C.
}
\tag{OE.4}
\]

Group the atomic train mass by the exact projective orbit

\[
\phi\mapsto\phi+{\pi\over3}\pmod\pi.
\]

Let `M_r` be the absolute train mass in orbit `r` and `E_r` its limiting
strict-rhombus crossing mass.  Audited §§142/168 give

\[
E_r\le {M_r^2\over4},
\qquad
\sum_rE_r=\Delta_2,
\qquad
\sum_rM_r=A.
\tag{OE.5}
\]

If

\[
M:=\max_rM_r,
\]

then

\[
4\Delta_2
\le\sum_rM_r^2
\le M\sum_rM_r=MA.
\]

Together with (OE.4),

\[
\boxed{
M\ge {4\Delta_2\over A}
\ge {4\Delta_2\over C}.
}
\tag{OE.6}
\]

Thus one three-direction orbit carries a macroscopically dominant set of complete
stopped trains.

---

## 3. The dominant orbit must carry many outer terminal occurrences

Freeze disjoint phase windows of radius `rho` around the at most three atoms of one
orbit attaining `M`, and then take sufficiently far finite sources.  The number of
complete trains outside those windows is at most

\[
(x-M+o_\rho(1))D.
\]

Every complete stopped train is a path and has exactly two terminal face incidences,
so it has at most two outer-face terminal occurrences.  The occurrence-counted outer
walk has `b` outer terminal occurrences in total.  Hence the number `K_D(rho)` of
outer occurrences carried by trains in the dominant-orbit windows satisfies

\[
K_D(\rho)
\ge b-2(T-MD)-o_\rho(D).
\tag{OE.7}
\]

Type-II perimeter convergence gives

\[
{b\over D}
={\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}+\delta
\longrightarrow\pi+\delta.
\]

Also

\[
x=C+{q\over2D}
=C+{t+\kappa\over2}.
\]

Therefore, after `D->infinity` and then `rho->0`, the normalized dominant-orbit outer
occurrence count `K` obeys

\[
K
\ge
\pi+\delta-t-\kappa-2C+2M.
\]

Using (OE.6),

\[
\boxed{
K
\ge
\pi+\delta-t-\kappa-2C+{8\Delta_2\over C}.
}
\tag{OE.8}
\]

The function

\[
-2C+{8\Delta_2\over C}
\]

is strictly decreasing for `C>0`.  Hence the upper bound (OE.2) gives the explicit
weaker but source-independent estimate

\[
\boxed{
K
\ge
K_*:=
\pi+\delta-t-\kappa-2U+{8\Delta_2\over U}.
}
\tag{OE.9}
\]

---

## 4. Round-hull transport converts orbit occurrences into detour energy

For consecutive loaded hull vertices `b_i,b_{i+1}`, let

\[
s_i=|b_i-b_{i+1}|,
\qquad
\ell_i=|W_i|,
\qquad
d_i=\ell_i-s_i\ge0.
\]

The distinguished routes partition the outer walk, so

\[
\sum_i d_i=d_{out}.
\tag{OE.10}
\]

Let `n_i` be the number of dominant-orbit-window outer edge occurrences on `W_i`.
Then `n_i<=ell_i`.  Set

\[
m_i:=\min\{n_i,s_i\}.
\]

Since

\[
(n_i-s_i)_+\le(\ell_i-s_i)=d_i,
\]

we have

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i m_i
\ge K_D(\rho)-d_{out}.
}
\tag{OE.11}
\]

Interpret `m_i` as fractional weight, at most one per selected occurrence, retained
from the dominant-orbit occurrences on route `i`.  Let `theta_i` be the projective
direction of the loaded-hull side `b_i b_{i+1}` and define the normalized side measure

\[
\mu_D={1\over D}\sum_i s_i\,\delta_{\theta_i}.
\]

The retained fractional weights define a submeasure

\[
\nu_D\le\mu_D
\]

whose total mass has lower limit at least `K-delta`.

The near-disk perimeter-measure convergence already used in audited §142 gives

\[
\mu_D\Rightarrow d\theta
\]

on the projective direction circle `R/pi Z`, where `dtheta` is Lebesgue measure of
total mass `pi`.  After subsequence extraction,

\[
\nu_D\Rightarrow\nu,
\qquad
0\le\nu\le d\theta,
\qquad
\nu(\mathbb R/\pi\mathbb Z)\ge K-\delta.
\tag{OE.12}
\]

Let `O` be the limiting three-point projective orbit and define

\[
g(\theta):=1-\cos d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(\theta,O).
\tag{OE.13}
\]

For an outer unit edge on route `i` whose train phase is within `rho` of `O`, its
exact directional-energy contribution

\[
1-u_i\cdot v
\]

is at least

\[
1-|u_i\cdot v|
\ge
1-\cos\bigl(d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(\theta_i,O)-\rho\bigr)_+.
\]

All route directional energies sum exactly to `d_out`.  Retaining only the fractional
weights from (OE.11), taking `D->infinity`, and then `rho->0` gives

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge\int g\,d\nu.
}
\tag{OE.14}
\]

This is the key new transport inequality: orbit concentration in train phase must be
paid against the uniformly rotating loaded-hull tangent measure.

---

## 5. Exact bathtub minimum for a three-direction orbit

The three points of `O` are equally spaced by `pi/3` on a projective circle of total
length `pi`.  For `0<=a<=pi/6`, the set of directions at distance at most `a` from
`O` has Lebesgue measure `6a`.

Among all submeasures `0<=nu<=dtheta` of prescribed mass `z`, the integral in
(OE.14) is minimized by filling the directions closest to `O`.  Consequently, for
`0<=z<=pi`,

\[
\boxed{
\int g\,d\nu
\ge F(z):=
6\int_0^{z/6}(1-\cos u)\,du
=z-6\sin(z/6).
}
\tag{OE.15}
\]

The function `F` is increasing on `[0,pi]`.  Combining (OE.12)--(OE.15) with (OE.9)
gives the necessary condition

\[
\boxed{
\delta
\ge
F\!\left(
\pi-t-\kappa-2U+{8\Delta_2\over U}
\right),
}
\tag{OE.16}
\]

whenever the displayed argument is positive.  Notice that the `+delta` in `K_*`
exactly cancels the `delta` capacity loss in (OE.11).

---

## 6. The entire low-`t` feasible triangle reduces to one endpoint

For fixed `t`, define

\[
k(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=
\pi-t-\kappa-2U+{8\Delta_2\over U},
\qquad
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{OE.17}
\]

The contradiction margin is

\[
H(t,\delta,\kappa):=F(k(t,\delta,\kappa))-\delta.
\tag{OE.18}
\]

The feasible region is

\[
\delta,\kappa\ge0,
\qquad U\ge C_0.
\]

First,

\[
{\partial k\over\partial\kappa}
={4\Delta_2\over U^2}>0,
\tag{OE.19}
\]

so `H` is minimized at `kappa=0` for fixed `t,delta`.

Second,

\[
{\partial k\over\partial\delta}
=4+{16\Delta_2\over U^2}<8,
\tag{OE.20}
\]

because `U>=C0>2sqrt(Delta2)`.  Also

\[
-2U+{8\Delta_2\over U}<0
\]

for `U>C0>2sqrt(Delta2)`, so throughout the old AG42-admissible region
`t>=t42`,

\[
0<k<\pi-t\le\pi-t_{42}=1.3207040156\ldots .
\]

Hence

\[
F'(k)=1-\cos(k/6)
\le0.024129,
\]

and therefore

\[
{\partial H\over\partial\delta}
=F'(k){\partial k\over\partial\delta}-1
<8(0.024129)-1<0.
\tag{OE.21}
\]

Thus `H` decreases with `delta`.  With `kappa=0`, the largest feasible detour is

\[
\delta={R(t)\over2},
\]

at which `U=C0`.  Therefore the weakest point of the entire feasible triangle is

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={R(t)\over2},
\qquad
U=C_0.
}
\tag{OE.22}

At that point put

\[
k_0(t)
:=\pi-t-2C_0+{8\Delta_2\over C_0}
\tag{OE.23}
\]

and

\[
H_0(t):=F(k_0(t))-{R(t)\over2}.
\tag{OE.24}
\]

Since

\[
H_0'(t)
=-F'(k_0(t))-{9\over4}<0,
\]

there is at most one scalar crossing.

---

## 7. New explicit floor

The Decimal/Taylor certificate gives

\[
H_0(1.82428)
=0.0000028087090542793\ldots>0.
\tag{OE.25}
\]

The scalar zero is

\[
t_*=1.8242812351707521395\ldots .
\tag{OE.26}
\]

Therefore every convergent low-`t` Type-II subsequence with

\[
t\le1.82428
\]

violates the necessary outer-energy condition (OE.16).  Combining with the old AG42
floor below `t42` yields:

### Theorem OE.1 — dominant-orbit outer-energy exclusion

Every full-core Type-II survivor sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}
\ge1.82428.
}
\tag{OE.27}

The theorem is deliberately stated at the round value `1.82428`, not at the numerical
root (OE.26).

---

## 8. Why this is different from the current penetration count

The convex-route/signed-penetration theorem proves more than `1.302D` distinct inward
contacts but leaves open concentration in a small number of macroscopic rhombic strips.
The present argument does not try to distribute those penetration contacts among
trains.  Instead it acts one level earlier:

```text
quadratic rhombus crossing density
 -> dominant three-direction atomic train orbit
 -> dominant orbit must own many outer terminal occurrences
 -> round force-hull tangent measure rotates uniformly
 -> those orbit occurrences pay directional energy
 -> total outer detour is too small at the old endpoint.
```

Thus the old endpoint is excluded even if all first-layer penetration contacts are
absorbed by a few long strips.

---

## 9. Scope and remaining obstruction

- The theorem is Type II/full-core only.
- It uses the proof-only AG42 constant `0.00042`; theorem-state authority is unchanged.
- Atomic windows are frozen after passage to one limiting train-phase measure; no
  atomic-mass semicontinuity assertion is used.
- The outer walk is occurrence-counted throughout.  A train has at most two outer
  terminal occurrences even when a physical bridge contributes two occurrences.
- The measure domination `nu<=dtheta` uses only `m_i<=s_i` and the same near-disk
  perimeter-measure convergence already used in audited §142.
- The projective energy bound uses `1-u_i dot v >= 1-|u_i dot v|`, so backward edge
  orientations cannot weaken it.
- Contact-isolated source points are retained in the full source and never enter the
  train ledger.

This does **not** close C-II.  For `t` moderately above `1.82428`, the network envelope
allows enough outer detour to pay the dominant-orbit tangent mismatch.  The next useful
step is to combine this new floor with the pentagon/outer-train population identities
and the signed penetration stack, rather than optimizing the old `t42` endpoint again.

## 10. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_dominant_orbit_outer_energy.py
```

for the high-precision scalar and monotonicity certificate.
